course: money
lesson: 2
title: Coins and notes: pounds and pence, dollars and cents
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Tip a piggy bank onto the carpet and you see a jumble of coins; the size of a coin does not tell you its value, the number stamped on it does.
explain: In the UK, 100 pence (p) make one pound (£), and the coins are 1p, 2p, 5p, 10p, 20p, 50p, £1 and £2; the notes are £5, £10, £20 and £50.
explain: In the USA, 100 cents make one dollar ($): a penny is 1 cent, a nickel 5 cents, a dime 10 cents and a quarter 25 cents, so four quarters make a dollar.
explain: To count coins fast, group the same coins, multiply, then add the groups; to check change, count up from the price to the money you gave.
explain: Real notes carry security features, like a see-through window and pictures that change when you tilt the note, so fakes are hard to make and easy to spot.
fact: In the UK, 100 pence make £1; the coins are 1p, 2p, 5p, 10p, 20p, 50p, £1 and £2.
fact: Size does not show value: the 2p coin is bigger than the £1 coin, but the £1 coin is worth fifty times more.
fact: In the USA, 100 cents make $1; a quarter is 25 cents, a dime 10 cents, a nickel 5 cents and a penny 1 cent.
fact: Ireland and many other European countries use the euro (€); 100 cents make one euro.
fact: In Australia the $1 and $2 coins are gold-coloured, so people call them "gold coins", though they contain no gold.
fact: "Quid" is slang for a pound, a "fiver" is £5 and a "tenner" is £10; "chancer" is Irish slang for someone who tries their luck.
fact: Change is the money you get back when you pay more than the price.
fact: Counting up from the price to the money you gave is the easiest way to check your change.
fact: Using the biggest coins first gives the fewest coins for any amount.
fact: Real notes have security features such as see-through windows and tilting pictures; fake money is worth nothing and making it is a serious crime.
fact: A price of £0.99 is 99p, only 1p less than £1; shops use prices ending in 9 because they feel cheaper.
exercise: Which UK coin is worth the most? | The £2 coin | The 50p coin | The copper 2p coin | The gold £1 coin
exercise: How many pence make one pound? | 100 | 10 | 50 | 1,000
exercise: Sophie has three 20p coins, two 10p coins and one 5p coin. How much is that? | 85p | 75p | 80p | 95p
exercise: Sophie's 85p buys an 80p pen. How much change does she get? | 5p | 15p | 10p | 0p
exercise: Noah's cousin pays for a $1 pretzel with quarters. How many quarters? | 4 | 2 | 5 | 10
exercise: How many cents is a dime worth? | 10 cents | 5 cents | 25 cents | 1 cent
exercise: Amy's brother offers £5 in coins for her £20 note. How much would she lose? | £15 | £5 | £10 | £20
exercise: Harry pays for a £1.35 drink with a £2 coin. What is his change? | 65p | 75p | 55p | 35p
exercise: What is the easiest way to check change? | Count up from the price to the money you gave | Count down from £100 to the price | Weigh the coins in your hand | Trust the till, it is never wrong
exercise: Why does Dad tell Grace to look for the see-through window on a note? | It is a security feature that real notes have | It shows how much money is left in the bank | It lets you see if the note is dirty | It shows the note is made of glass
exercise: In Australia, what is a "gold coin"? | A gold-coloured $1 or $2 coin | A coin made of real gold | Any coin found at the beach | A coin worth exactly $100
exercise: Jess wants to make 47p with the fewest coins. Which set is it? | 20p, 20p, 5p, 2p | 20p, 10p, 10p, 5p, 2p | 10p, 10p, 10p, 10p, 5p, 2p | Forty-seven 1p coins
exercise: Aidan buys chips for €3.50 with a €5 note. What change should he bring back? | €1.50 | €2.50 | €1.00 | €2.00
exercise: Mila says £0.99 is "basically nothing". How much less than £1 is it? | 1p | 9p | 10p | 99p
exercise: Which is worth more: three dimes or two quarters? | Two quarters (50 cents) | Three dimes (30 cents in all) | They are worth just the same | It depends on the size of the coins
exercise: What is a "tenner"? | A £10 note | A shiny 10p coin | Ten £1 coins in a bag | A £100 note
test: Your little cousin grabs the 2p cos "it's the biggest so it's the best innit". What's the truth? | Size doesn't matter, the 2p is worth less than every coin but 1p | Yes, bigger coins are always worth more | Yes, the 2p is worth more than the £1 | No, the smallest coin is always worth the most
test: Mate in New York goes, "gimme a dime and two nickels for this gum, it's 20 cents." Is that enough? | Yes, 10 + 5 + 5 = 20 cents | No, that's only 12 cents in all | No, that's only 15 cents altogether | Yes, and there's 5 cents change
test: Shopkeeper hands Jake change for £1.35 out of a £2 coin: a 50p and a 10p. He goes "hang on". Why? | That's 60p, but it should be 65p | That's 70p, which is too much | That's exactly right, 65p | That's 50p, which is too little
test: Your Aussie mate says "chuck us a gold coin for the sausage sizzle" ("sausage sizzle" is a charity barbecue). Sausage is $2. What do you give? | A $2 coin, or two $1 coins | A real gold coin from a museum | Two 20-cent coins | A $20 note, the gold one
test: What would happen if nobody's notes had any security features? | Fake notes would be easy to make and shops could lose money | Notes would last longer in your pocket | Prices in shops would all go down | Nothing, because nobody ever makes fake money
test: Bro counts his coins: "four 50ps, no wait, three 50ps and two 10ps." How much has he got? | £1.70 | £2.20 | £1.50 | £2.00
test: A Brummie mate goes "bostin', I got a twenty for me birthday!" ("Bostin'" means great.) How many £5 notes is a twenty worth? | 4 | 2 | 5 | 20
test: A game shows a "treasure chest" of 47 pennies and says it's "loads of money". What is it really worth? | 47p, less than half a pound | £47 | £4.70 | 47 cents is more than a pound
test: Ma in Dublin: "here's a tenner for the chipper, ya chancer, chips are €6.50." ("Chipper" is a chip shop.) What change? | €3.50 | €4.50 | €3.00 | €2.50
test: Mila's mum says a £0.99 price is a trick. What's the trick? | It feels much cheaper than £1 but is only 1p less | It means the shop gives 99p back | It is the price in another country's money | It means the item is nearly free
test: Harry's got a £5 note and a 50p. Snack's £3.80. Change? | £1.70 | £2.70 | £1.20 | £1.80
exam: State the number of pence which together constitute one pound sterling. | One hundred | Ten | Fifty | One thousand
exam: A purchaser tenders a two-pound coin in respect of an article priced at one pound thirty-five pence. Ascertain the change due. | Sixty-five pence | Seventy-five pence | Fifty-five pence | Thirty-five pence
exam: It is given that a quantity of three twenty-pence coins, two ten-pence coins and one five-pence coin is held. Determine the aggregate value thereof. | Eighty-five pence | Seventy-five pence | Ninety-five pence | Eighty pence
exam: Identify the principal reason for which banknotes incorporate transparent windows and images that alter upon tilting. | To render counterfeiting difficult and detection straightforward | To permit the holder to ascertain the balance of an account | To reduce the cost of production of each note | To indicate the date upon which the note shall expire
